610 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (The The Empty Bench project demonstrates how a short psychological mystery can be developed using one actor, two locations, minimal dialogue and a small number of narrative objects. The film uses the bench as a recurring visual symbol for absence, memory and attachment. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [2]
>>
endobj
611 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (The story begins with a mysterious message and gradually connects the present with a loss from three years earlier. The photograph and notebook provide concrete clues, while the unknown call and the protagonist's own voice create uncertainty about identity and perception. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
612 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (Cinematography, sound, silence and editing are treated as storytelling tools rather than decorative elements. In particular, the empty space beside the protagonist allows the audience to experience the absence without being shown a definite second character. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
613 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (The final scene returns to the bench and intentionally refuses to provide a single explanation. The film therefore remains faithful to its central idea that emotional attachment can make letting go more difficult than loss itself. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
614 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 150 0 R
/E (Overall, The Empty Bench provides practical experience in developing, filming and editing a short narrative while demonstrating how ambiguity, restraint and visual repetition can be used to communicate a psychological theme. )
/Pg 36 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
